body:
The `e.currentTarget === e.target` guard is fragile for nested UIs. When a user drags over a child element and then leaves the window from that child, `e.target` is the child ≠ `e.currentTarget` (the root div), so `setGlobalDragOver(false)` never fires and the overlay gets stuck.

The robust pattern is a **drag enter counter**:
```tsx
const dragCounterRef = useRef(0);

onDragEnter={(e) => {
  if (!e.dataTransfer.types.includes("Files")) return;
  e.preventDefault();
  dragCounterRef.current++;
  setGlobalDragOver(true);
}}
onDragLeave={(e) => {
  dragCounterRef.current--;
  if (dragCounterRef.current === 0) setGlobalDragOver(false);
}}
onDrop={(e) => {
  dragCounterRef.current = 0;
  setGlobalDragOver(false);
  // ...
}}
```

This is what VS Code, Figma, etc. use for drag-drop overlays.

Very minor: `setTimeout` without cleanup — if the component unmounts before the 4s fires, React will warn about setting state on an unmounted component. Not a real issue for the studio shell (it stays mounted), but if you want to be tidy:
```ts
const toastTimerRef = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>>();

const showUploadToast = useCallback((msg: string) => {
  clearTimeout(toastTimerRef.current);
  setUploadToast(msg);
  toastTimerRef.current = setTimeout(() => setUploadToast(null), 4000);
}, []);
```
This also gives you the bonus of resetting the timer if a second toast fires before the first clears. Non-blocking.

nit: This escaping is correct (`<\/script` breaks the HTML parser's closing-tag detection while JS evaluates `\/` as `/`), but it's non-obvious enough to trip up future readers. Consider a brief comment:

```ts
// Escape </script in downloaded content to prevent premature tag closure.
// <\/script is safe: HTML parser doesn't recognize it as a close tag,
// but JS treats \/ as / so the code executes identically.
```

Nice diagnostics. One thing to consider: `probeSession.page.evaluate()` can throw if the browser tab crashed or the page is in a bad state (which might be *why* duration is 0). The outer try/catch at line ~370 will catch it, but the error that surfaces would be the Puppeteer error ("Target closed" etc.) rather than the more helpful "Composition duration is 0" message.

Consider wrapping the diagnostics gathering in its own try/catch so a failed probe doesn't mask the real error:

```ts
let diagnostics: string[] = [];
try {
  if (probeSession) {
    const timelinesInfo = await probeSession.page.evaluate(() => { ... });
    // ... build diagnostics
  }
} catch {
  diagnostics.push("(Could not gather browser diagnostics — page may have crashed)");
}
```

**Concern: `<template>` content semantics may differ between cheerio and linkedom**

Per the DOM spec, `document.querySelectorAll()` does **not** match nodes inside `<template>` content — they live in a separate `DocumentFragment` accessible via `templateEl.content`. Cheerio flattens everything into one tree, so it _does_ match them.

If linkedom follows the spec here, the template composition inlining loop (`document.querySelectorAll("template[id]")`) would find the template elements themselves, but `templateEl.innerHTML` / reading content _inside_ the template may behave differently than cheerio's `.html()`.

The `isInsideTemplate()` helper suggests awareness of this — but it only matters if linkedom's `querySelectorAll` also reaches inside templates (like cheerio did). If it doesn't, the filter is unnecessary and the old cheerio-specific `$(el).parents().length > 0` check doesn't need a replacement.

**How to verify:** Add a small test or check in a REPL:
```ts
import { parseHTML } from "linkedom";
const { document } = parseHTML(`<html><body>
  <template id="foo-template">
    <div data-composition-id="foo">hello</div>
  </template>
  <div data-composition-id="foo"></div>
</body></html>`);

// Does this find the div INSIDE the template?
console.log(document.querySelectorAll('[data-composition-id="foo"]').length);
// If 2: linkedom flattens like cheerio → isInsideTemplate is needed
// If 1: linkedom follows spec → the template path works but differently

// Also check:
const tmpl = document.querySelector('template#foo-template');
console.log(tmpl.innerHTML);  // Does this return the template's content?
```

Also confirm your regression tests cover at least one composition that uses `<template id="X-template">` inlining.

**Concern: `insertAdjacentHTML` for scripts is less safe than `createElement` + `textContent`**

Elsewhere in this PR, styles are injected via `createElement("style")` + `.textContent = ...`, which avoids HTML parsing of the content. But scripts are injected via `insertAdjacentHTML`, which _does_ parse the string as HTML. If `compScriptChunks` content contains `</script>`, the parser will prematurely close the tag.

This is the same risk as the cheerio version (`$("body").append(...)`), so not a regression — but since you're touching this code anyway, using the safer pattern consistently would be an improvement:

```ts
const script = document.createElement("script");
script.textContent = compScriptChunks.join("\n;\n");
document.body.appendChild(script);
```

Non-blocking, just a suggestion for consistency with how styles are handled above.

**Critical:** `res.json()` is called without checking `res.ok`. When `caption-overrides.json` doesn't exist, the server returns 404. Calling `.json()` on a 404 response may return an HTML error page, causing a parse error that gets swallowed by the catch block.

Add a guard:
```ts
if (!res.ok) return;
const data = await res.json();
```

**Important:** Non-deterministic ID generation makes this untestable without mocking globals. Consider a counter-based approach or injectable ID generator:
```ts
let nextGroupId = 0;
const newGroupId = `group-split-${nextGroupId++}`;
```

🔴 **Bug: `syncRuntimeMedia` is called every tick — this will restart `load()` on every frame.**

Per the HTML spec, `HTMLMediaElement.load()` **aborts** any in-progress fetch and restarts from zero. If `syncRuntimeMedia` runs at 60fps while the media is unbuffered, it calls `load()` 60×/sec, resetting the fetch each time. The media may never finish buffering — a regression worse than the original bug (choppy first-play → no play at all).

This also accumulates `canplay` listeners (each tick creates a new closure), all of which fire simultaneously when the media finally loads.

**Suggested fix**: Track elements with a pending deferred play (e.g. a module-level `WeakSet<HTMLMediaElement>`) and skip this branch if already pending:

```ts
const pendingPlay = new WeakSet<HTMLMediaElement>();

// inside syncRuntimeMedia:
if (el.readyState >= HTMLMediaElement.HAVE_FUTURE_DATA) {
  void el.play().catch(() => {});
} else if (!pendingPlay.has(el)) {
  pendingPlay.add(el);
  if (el.preload !== "auto") el.preload = "auto";
  el.addEventListener("canplay", () => {
    pendingPlay.delete(el);
    if (!el.paused) return;
    void el.play().catch(() => {});
  }, { once: true });
  el.load();
}
```

🟡 **No error/timeout for failed loads** — If the media 404s, has a CORS error, or the network drops, `canplay` never fires and the media silently stays in a "waiting to play" state forever. The old code at least attempted `play()`, which would reject (caught by `.catch()`).

Consider also listening for the `error` event alongside `canplay`, or adding a timeout fallback:
```ts
el.addEventListener("error", () => {
  pendingPlay.delete(el);
}, { once: true });
```

🟡 **Test gaps**:

1. **`load()` not verified** — This test asserts `play()` is NOT called and `addEventListener("canplay")` IS called, but never checks that `el.load()` was called. That's the trigger that makes the fix work.

2. **Callback not exercised** — The test never fires the `canplay` event, so the callback logic (`!el.paused` guard, the actual `play()` call) is untested.

3. **Repeated-call scenario missing** — The most critical edge case — `syncRuntimeMedia` called multiple times while unbuffered — has no test. This is the scenario that exposes the `load()` restart bug.

Suggested additions:
```ts
it("calls load() on unbuffered media", () => { ... });
it("plays when canplay fires", () => { /* dispatch canplay event, assert play() called */ });
it("does not re-register listener if already pending", () => { /* call twice, assert one listener */ });
```

**Bug: promise hangs if base64 image is malformed.** `img.onload` resolves but there's no `onerror` handler. If the screenshot buffer is corrupt, this promise never settles and the validate command hangs.

```js
await new Promise(function (resolve, reject) {
    img.onload = resolve;
    img.onerror = function () { resolve(); }; // or reject — graceful skip is probably better here
    img.src = "data:image/png;base64," + imgBase64;
});
```

**Playwright browser leak.** `browser.close()` only runs on the happy path — if `pwPage.goto`, `waitForFunction`, `addStyleTag`, or any screenshot call throws, the outer `catch { return undefined }` swallows the error and the Chromium process is never closed. Wrap the page work in `try { ... } finally { await browser.close(); }`:

```ts
const browser = await chromium.launch({ ... });
try {
  const context = await browser.newContext({ ... });
  // ...all the existing work...
  return `screenshots/${filename}`;
} finally {
  await browser.close();
}
```

And consider pushing the error into `warnings` rather than silently returning `undefined` — right now a broken Playwright install produces zero signal to the user.

**Video-preview clip uses stale coordinates.** `v.top`/`v.left`/`v.width`/`v.height` were measured *before* scroll (lines ~528–540). After `window.scrollTo`, lazy loading / layout shifts can move the element, but we compute `clipY` from the pre-scroll `v.top`. For videos far down long pages this often captures the wrong rectangle (or empty space).

Re-measure after the scroll settles:

```ts
await page1.evaluate(`window.scrollTo(0, ${Math.max(0, v.top - 100)})`);
await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 300));

const rect = await page1.evaluate((filename) => {
  const v = [...document.querySelectorAll('video')]
    .find((x) => (x.src || x.currentSrc || '').includes(filename));
  return v ? v.getBoundingClientRect().toJSON() : null;
}, v.filename);
if (!rect || rect.width < 10) continue;

await page1.screenshot({
  path: previewPath,
  clip: { x: Math.max(0, rect.x), y: Math.max(0, rect.y),
          width: Math.min(rect.width, 1920), height: Math.min(rect.height, 1080) },
});
```

Separately: `v.play(); v.pause()` right before `page.screenshot` races the decoder — the screenshot fires before a frame is actually decoded, so you'll often capture the poster or a black frame. Either `await v.play()` + a small delay, or seek to `currentTime = 0.1` and wait for `seeked`.

**`duration` can be a function.** The fallback branch below correctly calls `tls[key].duration()` (GSAP timelines expose `duration` as a method). But this early return treats `win.__player.duration` as a value. If `__player.duration` is a function reference (truthy, but not a number), this returns the function itself, propagates up, and the `duration <= 0` check passes — you then compute `(i / (numFrames - 1)) * <function>` = `NaN`, and every snapshot seeks to NaN.

```ts
const d = win.__player?.duration;
if (d != null) return typeof d === 'function' ? d() : d;
```

**Path-traversal guard isn't cross-platform.** `projectDir + "/"` only works on POSIX — on Windows the separator is `\`, so legitimate requests inside the project fail the `startsWith` check and every file returns 403. Use `path.relative`:

```ts
const rel = relative(projectDir, filePath);
if (rel.startsWith('..') || isAbsolute(rel)) {
  res.writeHead(403); res.end(); return;
}
```

Same pattern should be lifted into a shared helper — `verify/index.ts` serves files from a local HTTP server too and its check is even looser (no traversal guard at all).

**Code injection via string interpolation.** `href` is interpolated directly into a string passed to `page.evaluate`. If a site serves a stylesheet URL containing `"` or `\`, this breaks execution or allows arbitrary JS injection in the browser context. Use parameterized evaluate instead:

```ts
await page.evaluate((targetHref) => {
  for (const link of document.querySelectorAll('link[rel="stylesheet"]')) {
    if (link.href === targetHref) { link.remove(); break; }
  }
}, href);
```

**Same `duration` function bug.** This is the same pattern flagged in the other inline review — `win.__player.duration` may be a GSAP method reference (truthy but not a number), which propagates to the seek positions as `NaN`. Same fix:

```ts
const d = win.__player?.duration;
if (d != null) return typeof d === "function" ? d() : d;
```

**POSIX-only path traversal guard — third instance.** Same pattern flagged on `capture/index.ts` and `verify/index.ts`. `projectDir + "/"` breaks on Windows where the separator is `\`. All three should share a single helper:

```ts
import { relative, isAbsolute } from "node:path";
function isInsideDir(base: string, target: string): boolean {
  const rel = relative(base, target);
  return !rel.startsWith("..") && !isAbsolute(rel);
}
```

**Path traversal — missing guard.** The fix applied to `snapshot.ts` (using `relative()` + `isAbsolute()`) was not applied here. A request to `../../etc/passwd` would read arbitrary files. The server only listens on `127.0.0.1` on an ephemeral port, so exploitation requires local access — but the pattern is identical to what was already fixed in `snapshot.ts`.

**Fix:** Add the same guard after the `join()` call:
```typescript
const rel = relative(projectDir, filePath);
if (rel.startsWith('..') || isAbsolute(rel)) { res.writeHead(403); res.end(); return; }
```

**Unbounded response body in Lottie interception.** `response.buffer()` downloads the entire response body into memory before the size check on the next line. A site serving a multi-GB response on a `.json` URL would OOM before the 5MB guard runs.

**Fix:** Check `Content-Length` header first when available:
```typescript
const cl = parseInt(response.headers()['content-length'] || '0', 10);
if (cl > 5_000_000) return;
```

`new URL(v.src)` + `url.pathname` can contain URL-encoded characters (e.g. spaces as `%20`). The local file server decodes request paths, but this reconstruction does not, so `existsSync(candidate)` will fail for valid files and skip injection. Decode the pathname (e.g. `decodeURIComponent`) before resolving to a filesystem path.
```suggestion
              // filesystem path from the decoded URL pathname.
              let filePath: string | null = null;
              try {
                const url = new URL(v.src);
                const pathname = decodeURIComponent(url.pathname);
                const candidate = resolve(projectDir, pathname.replace(/^\//, ""));
                const rel = relative(projectDir, candidate);
                if (!rel.startsWith("..") && !isAbsolute(rel) && existsSync(candidate)) {
                  filePath = candidate;
                }
              } catch {
                /* unresolvable src (e.g. blob:, data:, malformed encoding) — skip */
```

Addressed. The flag is now set **after** the await completes:

```typescript
} finally {
  lastBrowserConsole = domSession.browserConsoleBuffer;
  await closeCaptureSession(domSession);
  domSessionClosed = true;
}
```

If `closeCaptureSession` throws, control unwinds to the outer `finally` with `domSessionClosed === false`, so the defensive retry runs as intended. Verified the current order in `packages/producer/src/services/renderOrchestrator.ts` lines 2183–2184. Good catch — the original ordering would have masked exactly the failure mode this hardening was meant to catch.

