Issue: .catch handler missing inWindow guard

`hyper-shader.ts:308-320` — Guard post-capture DOM mutations with tl.time() window check to prevent scrub races guards .then with inWindow check because
late-resolving captures shouldn't mutate DOM for transitions playhead already
left. Same logic applies to .catch — but no guard here.

Scenario: scrub across multiple transitions in Safari with SVG-filter
compositions. Multiple captures fire, some reject late. Late rejection for
transition the playhead already passed fires [gsap.to](http://gsap.to)(fromEl, {opacity: 0}) and
gsap.fromTo(toEl, {opacity: 0}, {opacity: 1}) — this flashes toEl to 0 then
fades it to 1, even though the end-callback at T+dur already set it to 1. User
sees flash-to-black mid-scene.  

Fix is simple — wrap the crossfade in the same inWindow check:

```typescript
.catch((e) => {
  console.warn("[HyperShader] Capture failed, CSS crossfade fallback:", e);
  const nowTime = tl.time();
  const inWindow = nowTime >= T && nowTime < T + dur;
  if (inWindow) {
    const fromEl = document.getElementById(fromId);
    const toEl = document.getElementById(toId);
    if (fromEl && toEl) {
      gsap.to(fromEl, { opacity: 0, duration: dur, ease });
      gsap.fromTo(toEl, { opacity: 0 }, { opacity: 1, duration: dur, ease });
    } else {
      document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".scene").forEach((s) => {
        s.style.opacity = "0";
      });
      if (toEl) toEl.style.opacity = "1";
    }
  }
  if (wasPlaying) tl.play();
});
```

`root` is always the first `[data-composition-id]` in the document, even when `config.compositionId` is provided. That can cause `compId`, `data-width`/`data-height`, and `data-duration` to be read from the wrong composition when multiple compositions exist on the page. Consider selecting the root based on `config.compositionId` (e.g., query by the specific `data-composition-id`) and deriving width/height from that element.

Also, `Number(root?.getAttribute("data-width"))` / `data-height` can produce `NaN` for invalid values (or `0` for "0"), which will break canvas sizing/viewport; validate that parsed values are finite and > 0 and fall back to defaults otherwise.
```suggestion
  const parsePositiveDimension = (
    value: string | null | undefined,
    fallback: number,
  ): number => {
    const parsed = Number(value);
    return Number.isFinite(parsed) && parsed > 0 ? parsed : fallback;
  };

  const resolveCompositionRoot = (
    compositionId?: string,
  ): HTMLElement | null => {
    if (compositionId) {
      return (
        Array.from(document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-composition-id]")).find(
          (element) => element.getAttribute("data-composition-id") === compositionId,
        ) ?? null
      );
    }

    return document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-composition-id]");
  };

  const root = resolveCompositionRoot(config.compositionId);
  const compId = config.compositionId || root?.getAttribute("data-composition-id") || "main";
  const compWidth = parsePositiveDimension(root?.getAttribute("data-width"), DEFAULT_WIDTH);
  const compHeight = parsePositiveDimension(root?.getAttribute("data-height"), DEFAULT_HEIGHT);
```

🔴 **Reached from `DomEditOverlay`'s rAF loop every 16ms** (`DomEditOverlay.tsx:129`). For any class-selector selection this runs `doc.querySelectorAll` + `Array.from` + filter per frame, even when the user is idle.

Add a small resolver cache:
```ts
const lastResolvedRef = useRef<{ selection; el: HTMLElement } | null>(null);
// before querySelectorAll:
const cached = lastResolvedRef.current;
if (cached && cached.el.isConnected && matchesSelection(cached.selection, selection)) return cached.el;
```
and only re-walk on detach.

🔴 **Unbounded fetch — stalls the Studio dev server when Google is slow/blocked.**

No timeout, no `AbortController`. Behind a restrictive network, during a Google incident, or on the first request of a cold session with slow DNS, this handler hangs the dev API and the client's `useEffect([])` fetch blocks with it.

```ts
const controller = new AbortController();
const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 3000);
try {
  const response = await fetch(GOOGLE_FONTS_METADATA_URL, { signal: controller.signal });
  ...
} catch {
  return FALLBACK_POPULAR_GOOGLE_FONTS;
} finally {
  clearTimeout(timer);
}
```

