🟢 `_syncDetach` is never cleared when the caller invokes the returned unsubscribe. Sequence: `const detach = adapter.attachSync(comp); detach();` — the subscription is torn down inside `comp.on`'s handler set, but `this._syncDetach` still references the (now no-op) unsubscribe. On the next `attachSync(other)`, `this._syncDetach?.()` runs the already-detached unsubscribe. In practice this is idempotent — `comp.on`'s unsubscribe removes 0 handlers on the second call — but it's a shape that would silently break under a `comp.on` implementation that isn't. Cheap tightening:
```ts
const rawUnsub = comp.on("patch", ...);
const detach = () => {
  rawUnsub();
  if (this._syncDetach === detach) this._syncDetach = null;
};
this._syncDetach = detach;
return detach;
```
Bonus: the null check lets a caller `detach() → attachSync(other)` cleanly without a stale internal reference. — Rames D Jusso

🟠 *Coverage gap — `stageEnd` and `stageError` (L395) drop the `data` argument.*

Both `RenderObservabilityRecorder.stageEnd(phase, startedAtMs, data?)` and `stageError(phase, startedAtMs, error, data?)` accept an optional `data` third argument, but `observeRenderStage` does not pass it on either terminal path. Result: `status="end"` and `status="error"` events emit only `{phase, status, elapsedMs, durationMs, message}` — *no* `framesCompleted` / `totalFrames` / `captureMode` / `captureOperation` / `workerCount`.

That defeats the stall-diagnosis loop this PR is built for:

- Dashboards can chart `framesCompleted` *while* a stage is running (via checkpoint heartbeats) but **not `framesCompleted` at the moment the stage errored** — which is the exact number an operator wants when triaging a wedged render.
- Alerts filtering `capture_mode == "screenshot"` will not match `status="error"` events; screenshot-mode failures fall off screenshot-mode dashboards.
- Correlating `capture_operation` with `duration_ms` at end is impossible (`capture_operation` is absent on end).

Trivial fix:

```ts
try {
  const result = await fn();
  clearHeartbeats();
  recorder.stageEnd(phase, startedAt, data);      // ← add data
  return result;
} catch (error) {
  clearHeartbeats();
  recorder.stageError(phase, startedAt, error, data);  // ← add data
  throw error;
}
```

`captureStageObservationData()` getter object is safe to pass — `sanitizeObservationData` iterates via `Object.entries(data)`, which fires the getters at emit time, so `framesCompleted` at end is the true final count (no snapshotting concern).

This is the primary blocker per your coverage-audit ask — the heartbeat mechanism is correct, but the terminal events silence the same tags heartbeats expose.

— _[Review by Rames D Jusso](https://github.com/james-russo-rames-d-jusso)_

🔴 **Missing OR unquoted admission attrs silently default to 0 — approved video hoists with wrong timing.**

Root cause: `attrValueFrom` (L164-167) requires **double- or single-quoted** values (`(?:"([^"]*)"|'([^']*)')`). If the attr is missing entirely OR present but unquoted (valid HTML5), it returns `null`. Then `Number(null) === 0`, `Number.isFinite(0) === true` — passes the finite-check gate for `start` and `track`. Duration is saved by `duration <= 0` catching it, but `data-start` and `data-track-index` have no such lower-bound safety net.

Verified empirically against the hoist logic on this HEAD (`be38ac68`):

```
Case 1 — missing data-start:
<video data-frame-video="approved" src="c.mp4" data-duration="5" data-track-index="7"></video>
  → hoisted with start=0, errors=[]  (no error raised)

Case 2 — unquoted data-start=5:
<video data-frame-video="approved" src="c.mp4" data-start=5 data-duration="5" data-track-index="7"></video>
  → hoisted with start=0, cleanedAttrs="src=\"c.mp4\" data-start=5"  (the unquoted attr survives the strip too)
```

Case 2 also has a second symptom: the unquoted `data-start=5` is not stripped by `cleanedAttrs` (L196: the strip regex also requires quotes), so it passes through into the emitted host-root `<video>` — alongside the code's own `data-start="${globalStart}"` at L432. Result: **duplicate `data-start` attributes** on the emitted tag. Per HTML5 (first-declared wins), the browser reads the worker's raw `5` — **not the frame-translated globalStart**. So the video plays 5s off from where the timeline expects.

**Blast radius:** an AI-authored frame worker that forgets a `data-*` attr OR forgets to quote one produces a *silently mis-timed* approved video. The runtime happily plays it at t=0 of the frame (or the worker's raw untranslated seconds). The remaining `② HARD FAIL` scan at L219 blanks the hoisted comment and won't catch this — the video is already hoisted.

**Fix suggestions:**

**(A)** Distinguish "attr missing" from "attr = 0" using `null` explicitly:

```js
const rawStart = attrValueFrom(attrs, "data-start");
const rawDuration = attrValueFrom(attrs, "data-duration");
const rawTrack = attrValueFrom(attrs, "data-track-index");
if (rawStart === null || rawDuration === null || rawTrack === null) {
  errors.push(`${label}: approved frame video must declare quoted data-start, data-duration, and data-track-index`);
  return full;
}
const start = Number(rawStart);
const duration = Number(rawDuration);
const track = Number(rawTrack);
if (!Number.isFinite(start) || !Number.isFinite(duration) || duration <= 0 || !Number.isFinite(track)) {
  errors.push(`${label}: approved frame video attrs must be finite numbers, got start=${JSON.stringify(rawStart)} duration=${JSON.stringify(rawDuration)} track=${JSON.stringify(rawTrack)}`);
  return full;
}
```

**(B)** OR broaden `attrValueFrom` + the cleanedAttrs strip regexes to accept unquoted values (`(?:"[^"]*"|'[^']*'|[^\s"'=<>`]+)`) — this fixes the passthrough symptom of case 2 but doesn't fix case 1 (still `Number(null) === 0`).

(A) alone fixes both cases; (B) alone fixes case 2 only. (A) is what the admission-gate contract implies — "you didn't declare X, so we won't hoist."

**Coverage:** the new `media-contract.test.mjs` covers the happy path + the comment/script-scope negative. Add:
- Frame with `<video data-frame-video="approved">` missing `data-start` → expect `errors.length === 1`, no hoist.
- Frame with unquoted `data-start=5` → expect either error OR clean single-attr emission (whichever contract you land on).

