🟠 **`$` in a parent selector clobbers the `&`-nested composition.**

The second argument to `.replace` is a *replacement string*, not a raw string. Any `$1`, `$&`, `$$`, `$'`, `` $` `` inside `parentSelector` gets interpreted as a backreference.

Repro (`node -e`):
```js
const parent = '[data-x="$1"]';
const child = '& .b';
child.replace(/(^|[\s>+~,(])&/g, `$1${parent}`);
// → '[data-x=""] .b'   ← the parent's $1 expanded to the empty group-1 capture
```

The outer `$1` in the replacement template is the intentional capture. The `$1` inside `parentSelector` is unintentional. Result: an authored CSS attribute value containing `$` produces a silently-wrong composed selector, which then goes through `repeatedDescendantId` — the mangled output could either mask a legit repeat (id count changes) or fabricate one.

Rare in practice (CSS rarely uses `$` in attribute values), but a real correctness bug in a shared helper. Fix: either `parentSelector.replace(/\$/g, '$$$$')` before splicing, or use the function-form replacer:
```js
childSelector.replace(nestingToken, (_, sep) => sep + parentSelector);
```

